Capacity, Flow and Pressure Dynamics in the U.S. Unaccompanied Alien Children (UAC)
Care System
A Reproducible Data-Analytics Study Using Federal Operational Data (2023–2025)
Abstract
This paper presents a reproducible, end-to-end analytical study of daily caseload and flow dynamics in the
United States Unaccompanied Alien Children (UAC) program — the system operated by the Office of
Refugee Resettlement (ORR) within the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services (HHS), working
in sequence with U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP). The study analyses 720 daily observations
spanning 12 January 2023 to 21 December 2025, drawn from the ORR UAC Program public reporting
extract, and it does so across a single unbroken pipeline of cleaning, validation, feature engineering,
exploratory analysis, load-pressure classification, forecasting and dashboard delivery.
The study contributes five things. First, it documents a two-stage flow system in which CBP custody
operates as a short-horizon holding stock while HHS care operates as a long-horizon custodial population,
and it quantifies the resulting scale asymmetry: the mean HHS care population (6,061) is 35 times the
mean CBP custody stock (171), and HHS care alone carries 97.2% of total system load. Second, it identifies
a monotonic regime shift across the study window: mean total system load falls from 8,847 children in
2023 to 2,576 in 2025, a 70.9% contraction, with the single highest daily system load (11,762 children)
occurring on 20 December 2023 and the lowest (2,002) on 24 August 2025. Third, it constructs a
transparent, distribution-anchored load-pressure indicator — the intersection of the 75th-percentile
system-load threshold (8,249.5 children) with positive daily net intake — and finds that only 24 of 720
observations (3.3%) qualify, concentrated almost entirely in 2024 (22 of 24). Fourth, it evaluates five
forecasting models under a chronological 80/20 split and reports a deliberately uncomfortable result:
persistence beats every fitted model, with the naive last-value forecast reaching MAE 12.0, RMSE
15.1 and R² 0.993, while Holt's damped-trend model returns a negative R² (−1.396) and ARIMA(1,1,1)
returns −7.79 — a finding attributed to a structural level shift between the training and test regimes rather
than to any model defect, and one that is presented as a methodological warning rather than suppressed.
Fifth, it delivers the entire analysis as a deployed, interactive Streamlit dashboard so that non-technical
stakeholders can interrogate the same evidence the authors used.
The paper argues that the operational question for this system is not "how many children are in care" but
"how quickly is the flow being cleared relative to how quickly it is arriving." On this evidence, the second
quantity — throughput — has improved substantially over the study period even as the first has collapsed,
and the marginal value of further forecasting work in this dataset is currently low relative to its cost. It
closes with an explicit account of the study's limitations — irregular reporting cadence, stock-versus-flow
9/27/26, 12:19 PM Capacity, Flow and Pressure Dynamics in the U.S. Unaccompanied Alien Children (UAC) Care System
file:///C:/Users/LOQ/OneDrive/Desktop/UAC Data Care Analysis/reports/research_paper.html 1/38
definitional inconsistencies, non-uniform federal emergency-policy regimes, and a small, single-country,
single-agency data scope — and a set of ethical commitments governing any operational reuse of the work.
Keywords: unaccompanied children; ORR; HHS; CBP; caseload analytics; load pressure; capacity
planning; time-series forecasting; reproducible analytics; Streamlit.
1. Introduction
1.1 The operational question
Every federal care system faces the same arithmetic problem: demand arrives as a flow, but capacity is
provisioned as a stock. A system that admits children faster than it can resolve them accumulates a
backlog; a system that discharges faster than it admits drains. Most of the public discourse about the UAC
program has focused on the numerator — the number of children in care — largely because that is the
number that photographs well and legislates easily. This paper deliberately foregrounds the rate instead.
The Unaccompanied Alien Children program is an unusually well-suited subject for this reframing,
because it is a literal two-stage queueing network. Children first present to CBP, are held in short-duration
border custody, and are then transferred into the care of ORR and its network of private care providers,
where they remain until a case-resolution pathway is completed. The CBP stage is a transient buffer of a
few days; the HHS stage is a custodial population that persists for months. Analysing only the aggregate
obscures the fact that these are two populations with radically different dynamics, and that the binding
constraint is almost always the second.
1.2 What this study does
This study builds and evaluates a complete, reproducible analytical pipeline over federal operational data.
It is deliberately narrow in scope and deliberately complete in execution. The pipeline runs from raw file to
deployed dashboard with no manual steps between stages, and every intermediate artefact is written to
disk so that results are auditable rather than merely asserted.
Concretely, the study makes the following contributions:
1. A documented, executable cleaning pipeline that handles the specific failure modes present in
the ORR public extract, including comma-formatted numerals, reverse-chronological ordering, and a
large block of trailing null rows.
2. An explicit data-quality validation layer encoding three business-rule invariants (transfers
cannot exceed the stock they are drawn from; discharges cannot exceed the population they are drawn
from; counts cannot be negative) and reporting the 86 violations found.
3. A derived feature set translating raw stock-and-flow counts into eight analytically meaningful
indicators of system state and change.
