4. A load-pressure framework that converts the continuous caseload series into an actionable binary
classification of "the system is loaded and still absorbing net inflow."
5. An honest forecasting benchmark that reports model performance including, prominently, the
result that the benchmark was won by the no-model baseline.
6. A deployed interactive dashboard implementing the analysis for stakeholder consumption.
1.3 Structure of the paper
Sections 2 and 3 establish the institutional background and the prior analytical literature. Section 4 states
the research problem formally and Section 5 enumerates the objectives derived from it. Sections 6 through
10 document the data, preprocessing, validation, exploration and feature engineering stages. Sections 11
and 12 introduce the KPI framework and the load-pressure analysis. Sections 13 and 14 present the
forecasting exercise and its evaluation. Section 15 describes the deployed dashboard. Section 16
consolidates findings, Section 17 translates them into operational recommendations, and Sections 18
through 21 address limitations, ethics, conclusions and references.
2. Background
2.1 Institutional background
The UAC program is a federally funded, federally operated system for children who enter the United States
without a parent or legal guardian. Its structure has three components: ORR, which awards grants to care
providers and sets policy; CBP, which exercises statutory custody at the border; and the network of
contracted care providers — largely non-governmental organisations operating under ORR grants —
which deliver case management, shelter, education and legal services.
The statutory frame is the Trafficking Victims Protection Reauthorization Act of 2008, which established
statutory parameters for expedited removal of unaccompanied children and created the legal basis for
ORR's statutory care mandate. Executive Order 13867 (June 2018) reshaped processing and made initial
asylum screening by a specially-trained officer the default for children encountered by CBP, which
materially shortened average time in CBP custody. Subsequent policy changes, including the Title 42
public-health order (2020–2023) and the associated border regulation, caused extremely large
fluctuations in the observed caseload during the earlier part of this study's window and the phase-out of
those measures in 2023 coincide with the sharp decline documented here.
2.2 Why the data is analytically interesting
Three features make the UAC data set a productive and somewhat difficult analytical subject.
First, it is a two-stage flow network. Nearly all capacity analysis for single-stage systems must be rederived for this one. The mean CBP custody stock of 171.5 children against a mean HHS care population of
9/27/26, 12:19 PM Capacity, Flow and Pressure Dynamics in the U.S. Unaccompanied Alien Children (UAC) Care System
file:///C:/Users/LOQ/OneDrive/Desktop/UAC Data Care Analysis/reports/research_paper.html 3/38
6,061.3 makes the asymmetry stark: 97.2% of the total system caseload sits in the long-duration stage.
Second, the reporting cadence is irregular. The ORR extract contains observations on most but not
all calendar days. Over the 1,075-day study window, 720 dates are present and 355 are absent — 33.0% of
the calendar. Critically, the gaps are structured rather than random: of 719 inter-observation intervals, 558
are one day, 122 are three days, and 23 are four days. This is a reporting-cadence artefact, not a signal
about the underlying population, and treating the series as uniformly daily would be a methodological
error.
Third, the definition of a flow is ambiguous across stages. The 86 flagged records documented in
Section 8 are not arithmetic mistakes; they are almost certainly the product of HHS reporting daily
transfers from a stock snapshot that is itself measured on a different administrative cadence. This is a
definitional inconsistency, and it is treated as such.
2.3 Related work
Three literatures inform this study. The first is forecasting methodology and competitive
evaluation (Hyndman & Athanasopoulos, 2021; Makridakis, Spiliotis, & Assimakopoulos, 2018, 2022;
Petropoulos et al., 2022; Box, Jenkins, Reinsel, & Ljung, 2015). This literature has established, across
multiple large-scale competitions, the twin facts that (a) simple methods frequently outperform complex
ones, and (b) evaluation protocol — particularly the treatment of structural breaks — determines
conclusions more than model choice does (Tashman, 2000; Bergmeir & Benítez, 2012). This study's
forecasting result (Section 14) is a direct, if unusually stark, instance of (a) and (b), and the paper treats it
as corroborating rather than anomalous.
The second is practical time-series decomposition and anomaly detection, where Loess-based
decomposition (Cleveland, Cleveland, McRae, & Terpenning, 2020) provides the standard route to
separating trend from seasonality from remainder, and where robust statistics (Huber & Ronchetti, 2009)
provide the machinery for handling contamination. The third is the broader analytics engineering
and reproducible-research literature (Anscombe, 1973; Pedregosa et al., 2011; Harris et al., 2020;
McKinney, 2010; Hunter, 2007; Waskom, 2021; Wilson et al., 2017), which supplies the conventions this
study follows: explicit data-quality checks before inference, reproducible pipelines, and a separation of
exploration from confirmation.
3. Background: Analytical Context
3.1 Stock, flow, and the steady-state fallacy
The most common analytical error in capacity planning is to treat a cumulative count as a rate. The UAC
program publishes both, and the two behave completely differently. Over the study window:
The stock (children in care) fell by 70.9% in mean terms, 2023 → 2025.
9/27/26, 12:19 PM Capacity, Flow and Pressure Dynamics in the U.S. Unaccompanied Alien Children (UAC) Care System
file:///C:/Users/LOQ/OneDrive/Desktop/UAC Data Care Analysis/reports/research_paper.html 4/38
The flow (daily transfers into HHS care, mean 128.7) remained comparatively stable and, in 2024, the
cumulative flow balance was slightly positive (+863 children net) even as the stock fell sharply.
This is the signature of a system draining faster than it was filling — the desirable condition — rather
than a system in which children are being released. Any reading that conflates a falling stock with a rising
release rate will get this exactly backwards, and the paper returns to this point in Section 16.
3.2 The relationship between arrivals and throughput
The correlation structure supports the two-stage reading. Correlation with total system load is 0.92 for
HHS discharges, 0.73 for CBP transfers, 0.71 for apprehensions and 0.69 for CBP custody stock. The
stronger association with throughput than with arrivals is precisely what one expects if the caseload is
discharge-governed rather than admission-governed: HHS resolves a case, the child leaves the population,
and the population falls.
The lag structure reinforces this. Transfers out of CBP custody correlate with the following day's CBP
custody stock at r = 0.899, confirming that the flow variable leads the stock variable by one observation —
the expected signature of a queue being drained into a downstream stage. Discharges correlate
contemporaneously with the HHS population at r = 0.921.
3.3 What a useful dashboard must do
An operational dashboard that only plots the stock line is decorative. The value-add is in the derived
quantities: the net balance of inflow and outflow, the rate of change of the total, and a pressure
classification that answers the manager's actual question — "is this getting worse?" — with a defensible
threshold. The KPI framework of Section 11 and the pressure indicator of Section 12 are designed to supply
exactly those quantities.
4. Problem Statement
4.1 Formal statement
Let the UAC system on observation i be described by four stock and flow variables drawn from the ORR
extract:
A_i — children apprehended and placed in CBP custody on day i (arrivals, flow)
C_i — children in CBP custody on day i (stage-1 stock)
T_i — children transferred out of CBP custody on day i (stage-1 flow, into HHS)
H_i — children in HHS care on day i (stage-2 stock)
D_i — children discharged from HHS care on day i (stage-2 flow, out of system)
The research problem is stated as three questions.
9/27/26, 12:19 PM Capacity, Flow and Pressure Dynamics in the U.S. Unaccompanied Alien Children (UAC) Care System
file:///C:/Users/LOQ/OneDrive/Desktop/UAC Data Care Analysis/reports/research_paper.html 5/38
RQ1 (Composition). How is total system caseload distributed across the two custody stages, and how
has that distribution and its aggregate moved over the study window?
RQ2 (Pressure). On what observable conditions can the system be classified as being under load
pressure — a high caseload that is still absorbing net inflow — as distinct from merely high caseload or
merely positive inflow?
RQ3 (Predictability). To what extent is future total system load predictable from this data under a
defensible, chronologically honest evaluation protocol, and which model class — if any — adds value over a
persistence baseline?
4.2 Scope boundaries
This study is limited to the single public ORR extract named in Section 6. It does not use case-level or
person-level data, it makes no claim about any individual child's circumstances or outcomes, and it does
not attempt causal inference. The analysis is descriptive and predictive; it is not evaluative. Stating this
boundary up front is essential, because the temptation to read a caseload trend as a policy verdict is
precisely the inference error Sections 18 and 19 exist to prevent.
