The five count columns are presented as strings in the raw file, with thousands separators (e.g. "2,484" ).
This is the single most common ingestion failure mode for government statistical extracts and is handled
explicitly in Section 7.
6.4 Temporal coverage and reporting cadence
Observations are distributed across years as follows: 230 in 2023, 251 in 2024, 239 in 2025 — an
approximately even density that conceals a major structural change, as Section 9 shows. Coverage is not
complete, and the pattern of absence is informative:
Expected calendar dates in window: 1,075
Observed dates: 720 (66.97%)
Missing calendar dates: 355 (33.03%)
Inter-observation intervals longer than one day: 161 of 719 (22.4%)
Interval distribution: 1 day × 558; 2 days × 10; 3 days × 122; 4 days × 23; 5 days × 3; 6 days × 1; 7 days
× 1; 10 days × 1
The dominance of exactly 3-day gaps (122 occurrences) strongly suggests a scheduled reporting rhythm of
publication every third day, with daily publication added during the high-volume period. This is the
central reason the study models the series by observation index rather than by calendar day, and it is
carried forward as a first-order limitation (Section 18).
