Four observations follow immediately from Table 2 and are developed in Section 9.
1. The system is HHS-dominated. Mean HHS care (6,061) exceeds mean CBP custody (171) by a
factor of 35.3. Expressed as a share of total load, HHS care is 97.2% and CBP custody 2.8%.
2. The stock is far more dispersed than the flows. The coefficient of variation is 0.47 for
hhs_in_care and 0.74 for cbp_in_custody , but only 0.56 for the 7-observation total. The flows
( hhs_discharged CV 0.72, cbp_transferred_out CV 0.76) are proportionally more volatile than the
stocks they modify.
3. Net intake is negative on the majority of observations: 475 negative, 238 positive, 7 zero. The
cumulative balance over the full window is −32,212 children, consistent with a net contraction
across the three years.
4. The discharge_offset_ratio has a heavy right tail (max 43.27 against a median of 1.28) driven by
a small number of observations with near-zero transfers. This ratio is retained in the feature set but
flagged as sensitive at the denominator; it is excluded from the KPI framework in Section 11 for that
reason.
7. Data Preprocessing
Preprocessing is implemented twice in the repository, deliberately: once as a reusable library module
( src/data_cleaning.py , exposing clean_uac_data() ) and once as an executable narrative
( notebooks/01_data_cleaning.ipynb ) whose purpose is inspection. The duplication is discussed candidly
in Section 18; the pipeline executes seven steps.
9/27/26, 12:19 PM Capacity, Flow and Pressure Dynamics in the U.S. Unaccompanied Alien Children (UAC) Care System
file:///C:/Users/LOQ/OneDrive/Desktop/UAC Data Care Analysis/reports/research_paper.html 9/38
7.1 Null-row removal
The first operation is df.dropna(how="all") , which removes the 450 entirely-null trailing rows and
reduces the frame from 1,170 to 720 rows. This is a structurally-motivated deletion — these rows carry no
measurement of anything — and the count is reported rather than silently applied, in keeping with
Anscombe's (1973) principle that the reader must be able to see what was discarded.
7.2 Column standardisation
Raw column names are stripped of whitespace and mapped to snake_case via an explicit six-entry
dictionary (Table 1). The mapping is written as a literal dict rather than applied by a regex or a fuzzy
match, so that a future change to the source file produces a visible, diagnosable failure instead of a silent
misalignment.
7.3 Numeric coercion
Each of the five count columns is coerced through a four-step chain: cast to string → strip thousands
separators with str.replace(",", "", regex=False) → pd.to_numeric(errors="coerce") →
fillna(0).astype(int) . The regex=False flag is required: the default regex=True behaviour would treat
the comma as a pattern delimiter. The fillna(0) step is the one genuinely contestable choice in the
pipeline and is examined in Section 8.
7.4 Date parsing and ordering
Dates arrive as strings of the form "December 21, 2025" and are parsed with pd.to_datetime . Because
the source is in reverse chronological order and the first row is not the earliest date, the frame is then
sorted ascending and re-indexed with reset_index(drop=True) . Omitting this sort is the second most
likely ingestion error after the comma-handling issue, and it would silently reverse every time-series
operation downstream.
7.5 Path resolution
The library resolves the raw file across four candidate locations (project root, current working directory,
an alternate local path, and the user's Downloads folder) before raising a FileNotFoundError with an
actionable message. This is a defensive measure against the very common failure in notebook-heavy
projects where the working directory differs from the project root. It does not affect analytical results.
7.6 Initial derived fields
Three fields are created in the library at this stage: hhs_net_daily_change (=T_i - D_i), and calendar fields
year , month and day_of_week . The remaining derived features are produced by the feature-engineering
stage (Section 10) and written to uac_features.csv .
