9.2 Stage composition
Figure 2
Figure 2. Caseload by custody stage. The HHS line sits two orders of magnitude above the CBP line on
any linear scale — itself a finding, since it means CBP-stage movements are visually invisible in a
combined plot. The analytical consequence is that no useful combined index can be plotted linearly; the
two stages must be read separately or on a log scale.
Both stages fall together, and they fall together fast: CBP custody declines more sharply in proportional
terms than HHS care, consistent with a policy-driven contraction of border-stage processing rather than of
the care caseload itself.
