9.3 Flow dynamics
Figure 3
Figure 3. (a) Daily throughput — transfers out of CBP custody and discharges from HHS care; (b) net
intake, red where positive (inflow exceeds outflow) and blue where negative.
Throughput tracks stock closely, as the correlation structure of Section 3.2 predicts (discharges vs HHS
stock r = 0.921; transfers vs next-day CBP stock r = 0.899). The net-intake panel is the analytically
valuable one: it is a signed series whose cumulative value distinguishes system expansion from system
drainage regardless of the absolute stock level.
Cumulative net intake over the full window is −32,212 children, composed of −30,120 in 2023, +863 in
2024 and −2,955 in 2025. The 2023 figure reflects the peak build; the 2024 figure is the only year in which
the system absorbed slightly more than it released, and it is worth noting that this occurred while the stock
was falling — 2024 net intake of +3.4 per observation on a mean load of 7,320 coexists with a 17.2%
annual fall in mean stock. The two facts are only reconcilable because throughput was rising faster than
inflow; the aggregate stock fell even while the balance was marginally positive.
