9.4 Monthly structure
Figure 5
Figure 5. (a) Monthly mean caseload decomposed by custody stage; (b) monthly cumulative net intake.
Monthly aggregation shows the same 2023→2025 trajectory compressed, with a visible seasonal
component superimposed. The monthly net-intake series changes sign repeatedly in 2023 and 2024
before settling persistently negative through 2025, indicating that by the final year the system was
draining in every observed month rather than oscillating.
9.5 Day-of-week structure — reported, then discounted
Mean total load by day of week ranges from 4,407.5 (Friday) to 6,334.7 (Wednesday). This apparent
30% weekday effect should not be interpreted as a staffing or arrivals rhythm, for two reasons
that the analysis itself supplies. First, the reporting cadence is not daily (Section 6.4), so weekday
assignment is confounded with publication rhythm — a day that appears rarely in the calendar appears
disproportionately at certain points in the cycle. Second, and decisively, the Friday minimum is almost
entirely a composition effect: the mean is computed over a period in which the level collapsed by 70%, and
later years — with a different mix of weekdays among their surviving observations — dominate the low
end. A proper test would require a de-trended or within-year weekday analysis, which this dataset's
cadence does not cleanly support.
The honest statement is: a weekday effect is present in the marginal means and is confounded with both
reporting cadence and the secular level shift; this study does not claim a weekday effect. This is recorded
in Section 18 as an example of a plausible-looking pattern deliberately not asserted.
