12. Load Pressure Analysis
12.1 Definition
The pressure construct combines the two conditions that jointly define operational strain. A high caseload
alone is not pressure — a large, well-resourced system holding 9,000 children is not necessarily under
strain. Positive net intake alone is not pressure either — a small system growing from 200 to 220 children
is not under strain. Pressure is the conjunction.
$\text{LoadPressure}_i = \mathbb{1}\!\left[\text{TotalSystemLoad}_i \ge Q_{75}\right] \cdot
\mathbb{1}\!\left[\text{NetIntake}_i > 0\right]$
The 75th-percentile threshold is computed in-sample and equals 8,249.5 children. It is used rather
than an absolute capacity figure because the source contains no published bed-capacity or staffing
denominator, and inventing one would fabricate the most decision-relevant parameter in the entire
analysis. The percentile threshold is a relative strain indicator — "loaded relative to this system's own
recent history" — and Section 12.5 states explicitly what it is not.
12.2 Distribution of the three indicators
Indicator Count Share of 720
High load (≥ Q75 = 8,249.5) 180 25.00%
Positive net intake (> 0) 238 33.06%
Load pressure (both) 24 3.33%
The intersection is 3.3% of observations. Positive net intake is only modestly more common than the
~23% expected under a null of random independence between the two conditions, indicating that the two
indicators are close to independent in this sample — high load does not systematically coincide with net
inflow.
9/27/26, 12:19 PM Capacity, Flow and Pressure Dynamics in the U.S. Unaccompanied Alien Children (UAC) Care System
file:///C:/Users/LOQ/OneDrive/Desktop/UAC Data Care Analysis/reports/research_paper.html 22/38
12.3 Temporal structure of pressure periods
Year High-load obs. Positive-intake obs. Pressure obs.
2023 128 87 2
2024 52 100 22
2025 0 51 0
The temporal structure is the finding. All 128 high-load observations in 2023 produced only 2
pressure days, because 2023's inflows were overwhelmingly negative — the system was building a backlog,
and the flows show it. Conversely, 2024 produced 22 of the 24 pressure days despite containing only 52
high-load observations, because 2024 was the one year with a net-positive intake balance. In 2025 neither
condition is met, and pressure events vanish entirely.
Pressure periods are therefore not a property of caseload magnitude. They are a property of caseload
magnitude combined with adverse flow direction, and that combination occurred in a specific
window — predominantly 2024 — and has since ceased.
Figure 4
Figure 4. Load-pressure periods. Grey points are below the Q75 threshold, red points are high-load days,
and ringed points (n = 24) satisfy both conditions. The visual confirms that the pressure subset is not a
contiguous episode but scattered single observations within a broader high-load band.
12.4 Characterisation of pressure days
The 24 pressure observations share a common signature: they occur on high-load days that nevertheless
register small positive net intake. The mean net intake on pressure days is small relative to the flow
magnitudes involved, indicating that these are not days of acute surge but days on which a heavily loaded
system failed to make net progress. This distinction matters operationally: a surge response (adding
capacity) and a marginal-drain response (accelerating existing throughput) are different interventions,
9/27/26, 12:19 PM Capacity, Flow and Pressure Dynamics in the U.S. Unaccompanied Alien Children (UAC) Care System
file:///C:/Users/LOQ/OneDrive/Desktop/UAC Data Care Analysis/reports/research_paper.html 23/38
and the pressure construct is designed to flag the second, which is the harder and more common
operational problem.
12.5 Limits of the construct
Four limitations are stated so that the indicator is not over-read.
1. The threshold is in-sample and non-stationary. A fixed 8,249.5 is a high bar in 2025 (when the
maximum observation is roughly 3,400) and a low bar in 2023. Because the system contracted 70.9%,
the in-sample Q75 is dominated by 2023–2024 conditions. Re-deriving the threshold per year would
change the 2025 classification from "no pressure" to "most days are pressure" — the indicator is
meaningless across the regime shift unless re-based. This is a genuine weakness, and the reason K8
carries an explicit re-derivation warning in Table 8.
2. No absolute capacity benchmark exists. Without a published bed count, staff ratio, or licensed
capacity, this measures relative strain only. It cannot answer "is the system over capacity," which is the
question managers actually ask.
3. Net intake is a same-day difference of noisy quantities. A value of +3 on a base of 2,500 is
within the noise of the reporting cadence and is not a meaningful inflow. The binary cut at > 0 treats
+3 and +206 identically, which it should not.
4. The indicator has no persistence criterion. A single day's conjunction is thin evidence of strain.
Consecutive-day or sustained-window criteria would be more robust and are a natural extension.
13. Forecasting
13.1 Design
Forecasting is conducted on total_system_load as a univariate series indexed by observation order,
not calendar date (Section 6.4). Data preparation drops missing values, re-sorts chronologically, and
produces 720 model-ready observations.
The evaluation protocol is a chronological 80/20 split with no shuffling:
Partition Observations Role
Training 576 (80.0%) Model fitting
Test 144 (20.0%) Held-out evaluation
A chronological split is used rather than random cross-validation because random folds leak future
information into training, which produces optimistically biased accuracy estimates in time series
(Bergmeir & Benítez, 2012; Tashman, 2000). This study considers the leakage-free protocol nonnegotiable and treats the resulting numbers as valid even though they are unflattering.
9/27/26, 12:19 PM Capacity, Flow and Pressure Dynamics in the U.S. Unaccompanied Alien Children (UAC) Care System
file:///C:/Users/LOQ/OneDrive/Desktop/UAC Data Care Analysis/reports/research_paper.html 24/38
13.2 Models evaluated
Five model classes are evaluated, spanning increasing complexity:
1. Naive (persistence) — \hat{y}_{t} = y_{t-1}. The benchmark every other model must beat.
2. Historical mean — constant at the training mean.
3. Drift — linear interpolation between the first and last training observations.
4. Holt's linear / Holt's damped-trend exponential smoothing — level and trend, with the
damped variant imposing a decelerating trend.
5. ARIMA(1,1,1) with linear trend — differenced autoregressive model, fitted by maximum
likelihood.
13.3 Metrics
Four complementary metrics are reported. MAE and RMSE are scale-dependent and expressed in
children. MAPE and MAE-percent are scale-free and are the operationally meaningful quantities, since
a 10% error against a caseload of 2,500 is a very different operational event from a 10% error against
10,000. R^2 is reported because the sign of R^2 is the most compact possible statement of whether a
model beats a mean predictor — and because in this study the R^2 signs are the story.
14. Model Evaluation
