Charts. Four Plotly visualisations, all computed on the filtered frame:
1. total_system_load line — the caseload trajectory (Figure 1).
2. CBP custody vs HHS care dual line — stage composition (Figure 2).
3. Transfers vs discharges dual line — throughput comparison (Figure 3a).
4. net_intake bar chart — pressure direction, coloured by sign (Figure 3b).
Data quality panel. A dedicated subheader reports the count of flagged records ( data_quality_flag
sum) and carries the explicit statement that the dataset contains missing calendar dates and that missing
dates are not assumed to represent zero activity. This places the Section 8.7 warning directly in front of
every dashboard consumer rather than in an appendix.
15.2 Design rationale
The dashboard's structure is a direct expression of the paper's argument. It leads with state (K1–K3 as
metric tiles) because that is what a manager opens the page for; it follows with composition (the dualstage chart) because the two-stage structure is the most commonly misunderstood feature of the system; it
then presents direction (the net-intake bars) because direction, not level, determines whether conditions
are deteriorating; and it closes with data quality, because no interpretation above is valid without it. The
ordering is not arbitrary — it follows the argument of Section 3.3.
15.3 Deployment and reproducibility
Requirements are pinned to exact versions in requirements.txt (pandas 3.0.6, numpy 2.5.3, plotly 7.1.0,
streamlit 1.64.0, scikit-learn 1.9.1, statsmodels 0.15.0, matplotlib 3.11.2, seaborn 0.13.2, Jupyter 1.1.1),
making the environment reproducible. The application runs locally via streamlit run app.py . Six
publication-quality figures are generated to reports/figures/ and are embedded in this paper.
