16.1 The central finding
Behind the ten findings sits one conclusion, and it is the paper's principal contribution to the operational
conversation:
The right question about the UAC care system is not "how many children are in care" but
"is the system clearing its backlog faster than it is building one." On this evidence, throughput
has improved substantially. The stock fell 70.9% while discharge throughput held broadly stable and the
cumulative net intake balance was negative in two of three years. The dominant risk in this dataset is not
accumulation; it is the possibility that a reduction in measured caseload is being read as a reduction in
pressure on children, when the causal chain actually runs through faster resolution.
9/27/26, 12:19 PM Capacity, Flow and Pressure Dynamics in the U.S. Unaccompanied Alien Children (UAC) Care System
file:///C:/Users/LOQ/OneDrive/Desktop/UAC Data Care Analysis/reports/research_paper.html 30/38
This distinction is not rhetorical. It determines whether an analyst recommends adding capacity (correct if
the system is accumulating) or accelerating case resolution (correct if it is draining slowly) or neither
(correct if it is in balance). K1 cannot distinguish these cases. K4 can.
17. Operational Implications
17.1 For capacity planners
Re-base all thresholds; do not carry them across the regime shift. The in-sample Q75 of 8,249.5
is meaningless in a regime where the maximum observation is ~3,400. Any dashboard, alert or SLA
carrying a fixed caseload threshold inherited from 2023–2024 will either never fire (as here) or fire
constantly if re-based naively. Thresholds should be re-derived annually from trailing data, and the
derivation rule should be documented alongside the threshold.
Plan against net intake, not stock. The difference between a system holding 6,000 children and
draining 45 per day and a system holding 6,000 children and gaining 45 per day is the difference between
two very different futures. K4 provides this at a glance.
Instrument the stage boundary. The 86 transfer-conservation violations indicate that CBP and HHS
are not measuring the stage-1 stock on a cadence compatible with measuring the stage-1 flow. This is a
small data-engineering fix with real operational value: once the boundary is measured consistently, the lag
between presentation and placement becomes directly measurable, and that lag is a genuine quality-ofservice metric.
17.2 For the data and analytics function
Do not forward-fill this series. The single most consequential analytical decision in this project was
declining to fill the 355 missing dates. Any pipeline that resample('D') and sums will produce a mean
caseload roughly a third too low and will report a collapse that did not occur. The dashboard states this
explicitly and should continue to.
Retain the data-quality flags end-to-end. The data_quality_flag column should survive into every
downstream consumer. Its 12% rate is not an embarrassment; it is a documented, characterised, non-fatal
property of the source.
Reconcile the duplicated pipeline. The project currently maintains two implementations of the
cleaning logic ( src/data_cleaning.py and notebooks/01_data_cleaning.ipynb ) producing two cleaned
artefacts. They are currently equivalent, but the notebook is the one the author actually inspected and the
library is the one the dashboard depends on downstream. This should be collapsed to a single source of
truth, with the notebook reduced to import-and-inspect.
Treat the forecasting capability as dormant. Section 14's finding is that the current modelling does
not earn its maintenance. The valuable next investments are not better models but better data: a denser
9/27/26, 12:19 PM Capacity, Flow and Pressure Dynamics in the U.S. Unaccompanied Alien Children (UAC) Care System
file:///C:/Users/LOQ/OneDrive/Desktop/UAC Data Care Analysis/reports/research_paper.html 31/38
series, a consistent stage-boundary definition, and a documented structural-break history.
17.3 For policymakers and the public
The 2023→2025 decline should be read as a throughput story. Mean caseload fell 70.9%.
Cumulative net intake was −32,212 across the window. The system's discharge throughput tracks the
caseload more strongly than arrivals do (r = 0.920 vs 0.710). The most defensible plain-language summary
is that the system resolved cases faster over this period than it accumulated them — while noting, per
Section 18, that this study can describe the pattern but cannot attribute it to any particular policy.
Publish a capacity denominator. The single largest analytical limitation of this study is the absence of
any published bed count, licensed capacity, or staffing ratio. A federal system that published its capacity
denominator alongside its caseload would make pressure classification in Section 12 transformable from a
relative measure into an absolute one, and would make the forecasting question in Section 14 considerably
more answerable. This is a recommendation about data publication, and it is the highest-leverage one in
this paper.
18. Limitations
The limitations below are stated at length because several of them bound the conclusions more tightly
than any methodological choice made in this study.
L1 — Structural break dominates all results. The 68.4% level difference between the training and
test partitions is the dominant feature of the forecasting evaluation and the in-sample Q75 threshold.
Results in Sections 12 and 14 should not be transferred to other periods, and the Q75 threshold in
particular does not survive the regime shift.
L2 — Single split, no uncertainty estimate. All Section 14 numbers come from one 80/20
chronological split. No confidence interval, no rolling-origin replication. Given that the entire performance
ordering depends on split placement, these figures are descriptive of this split and should be treated as
such.
L3 — Irregular reporting cadence. 33.0% of calendar dates are absent, and gaps are structured (558
× 1 day, 122 × 3 days). All rolling windows are observation-based, not day-based, and thus denote variable
calendar spans. This limits the interpretability of every "7-day" construct in the feature set, which is really
a "7-observation" construct spanning a median of 10–12 days.
L4 — Stock-versus-flow definitional inconsistency. 86 records violate transfer conservation, and
the violations are structural rather than random. Any derived quantity depending on CBP stock as a
binding constraint is unreliable on those days. The cause is inferred, not confirmed, because the extract
does not carry the source footnotes.
9/27/26, 12:19 PM Capacity, Flow and Pressure Dynamics in the U.S. Unaccompanied Alien Children (UAC) Care System
file:///C:/Users/LOQ/OneDrive/Desktop/UAC Data Care Analysis/reports/research_paper.html 32/38
L5 — No absolute capacity benchmark. No bed count, staffing ratio, caseworker caseload standard or
licensed capacity is available. The pressure construct in Section 12 is therefore strictly relative. It cannot
answer whether the system was over capacity, which is the question that matters most.
L6 — Undocumented source footnote. The asterisk on the arrivals column denotes a source caveat
whose text is not reproduced in the extract. The operational definition of "apprehended and placed in CBP
custody" is therefore inferred rather than known.
L7 — Duplicated pipeline. Cleaning logic exists in two places and produces two artefacts. They agree
today; nothing guarantees they will.
L8 — Aggregate-only data. No case-level, demographic, geographic or case-resolution-pathway data is
used. The analysis cannot speak to case outcomes, to differences between children, or to anything below
the aggregate. Any such claim would be unsupported.
L9 — No causal inference. Every relationship reported is associational. The system declined over a
period in which federal border and asylum policy changed substantially, but this study does not identify,
isolate or estimate the effect of any policy on any outcome. Attributing the decline to any cause is out of
scope and unsupported by this design.
L10 — Two undocumented features. net_intake_7obs is populated on only 254 of 720 observations
and is excluded from the KPI framework. discharge_offset_ratio is undefined at T_i = 0 and extreme at
small denominators. Both are documented in Section 10 rather than silently retained.
L11 — A plausible pattern deliberately not asserted. The day-of-week means range 30% (Section
9.5) and look like a staffing rhythm. This study does not claim a weekday effect, because the pattern is
confounded with both the reporting cadence and the secular level shift and cannot be cleanly separated in
this data. It is reported here as a limitation of the analysis rather than a result of it.
L12 — Forecasting scope. Five univariate model classes were evaluated. No multivariate, machinelearning (Breiman, 2001; Ke et al., 2017; Hastie, Tibshirani, & Friedman, 2009), ensemble, or regularised
(Tibshirani, 1996) approaches were tried, and no exogenous predictors — policy dates, border encounters,
seasonal or climatic covariates — were incorporated. The conclusion in Section 14 is that these models do
not add value here, not that no model could.
L13 — Extrapolation is not warranted. The 2025 low-load regime is within the sample. Nothing in
this study supports projecting 2026 conditions, and the extreme values of the 2023 peak should not be
treated as a plausible upper bound for a future high regime.
L14 — Source volatility. The ORR extract is a live federal operational publication. Its extraction
method, revision policy and coverage may change without notice. All findings are conditioned on the
extract as retrieved.
