19. Ethical Considerations
E1 — Privacy is structurally secured, not merely respected. The dataset contains daily aggregate
counts only: no names, no case identifiers, no demographic breakdowns, no geographic granularity finer
than national, and no free text. There is no re-identification pathway from this data to any individual child,
and none was attempted. This is stated affirmatively because in applied child-services analytics, the
structural absence of identifiers is a stronger guarantee than a policy commitment not to misuse them.
E2 — The paramount obligation is to children, not to the dataset. The children represented in
these rows are, by definition, unaccompanied minors in federal custody, frequently separated from family,
frequently in proceedings they cannot influence. The Belmont Report (1979) — respect for persons,
beneficence, justice — applies in full, and the study's handling of that obligation is concrete rather than
rhetorical. The dashboard exists to help the system serve children better; a deployment in which it were
used to justify reduced capacity would violate the beneficence principle regardless of how accurate the
statistics were.
E3 — The single greatest ethical risk in this work is the direction of inference. A dataset
showing a 70.9% decline in children in federal care invites the reading that the system is improving. This
study argues the opposite of that reading: that the decline reflects faster resolution, which is a measure of
system throughput, not of child wellbeing. Resolution speed is an administrative property. Whether the
children being resolved had good outcomes, were safely placed, or were simply removed from the count is
not knowable from this data and must not be inferred from it. This caution is the reason Section
17.3 pairs the throughput finding with an explicit statement that the study cannot attribute the decline to
any policy.
E4 — Non-stigmatisation of a highly stigmatised population. The population under study is a
group on whom border-policy discourse routinely projects contested framings. This paper uses neutral
technical language throughout ("net intake", "throughput", "resolution") rather than the charged
vocabulary common in public debate, and it makes no normative claim about immigration policy, border
policy, or the merits of any detention or release decision. The analytical object is a system's
throughput, and it would be a category error to read a throughput finding as an endorsement.
E5 — No individual-level actionability. Nothing in this analysis supports action against any
individual, family, region or provider. The unit of analysis is the national daily aggregate. Any use of these
outputs to characterise a specific locality, a specific provider, or a specific cohort would be an invalid
extrapolation and should be treated as a misuse of the work.
E6 — Transparency and reproducibility as an ethical requirement. The pipeline is
deterministic, version-pinned, and fully inspectable; the six figures and every tabulated statistic in this
paper are regenerated from the raw extract by re-running it. This is not merely good practice: a study that
shapes resource allocation for a vulnerable population must be independently checkable by people who
disagree with it, and by people who have no access to the underlying individual data. Reproducibility is
what makes that possible.
9/27/26, 12:19 PM Capacity, Flow and Pressure Dynamics in the U.S. Unaccompanied Alien Children (UAC) Care System
file:///C:/Users/LOQ/OneDrive/Desktop/UAC Data Care Analysis/reports/research_paper.html 34/38
E7 — Honest reporting includes adverse results. Section 14 reports that the persistence baseline
beat every fitted model, and Section 12.5 reports that the headline pressure indicator does not survive the
regime shift. Suppressing either would have produced a paper that read as more successful and would
have been less useful and less honest. A favourable finding obtained by a choice of protocol is not a
finding.
E8 — Dual-use and downstream misuse. An aggregate caseload forecast could in principle be
misused to anticipate population movements. The magnitude of that risk here is low — the series is a
three-year retrospective national aggregate, published by a federal agency, already in the public domain —
but the obligation to note it is real, and it argues for the study's practice of reporting uncertainty and
regime-dependence rather than a single headline projection.
E9 — Interpretive humility. Where the data cannot answer a question — the true cause of the transfer
violations, the absolute capacity of the system, the wellbeing of any child, the effect of any policy — this
paper says so. The persistent risk in applied analytics is the confident sentence that outruns the evidence,
and Section 18's length is itself a statement of where the boundary sits.
20. Conclusion
This study built a complete, reproducible analytical pipeline over 720 days of federal operational data
describing the U.S. Unaccompanied Alien Children care system between January 2023 and December
2025, and asked three questions of it: what is the composition of the caseload, when is the system under
pressure, and how predictable is it?
The composition question produced a clear structural answer. The system is a two-stage flow network in
which the long-duration stage carries 97.2% of the caseload, and in which caseload is more strongly
associated with discharge throughput (r = 0.920) than with arrivals (r = 0.710). Over the window the total
caseload fell 70.9%, from a mean of 8,846.5 to 2,575.7, with the sharpest step-down between 2024 Q4 and
2025 Q1.
The pressure question produced a 3.3% answer with a sharp temporal structure. Only 24 of 720
observations combined a high caseload with positive net intake, and 22 of those fell in 2024 — a year in
which the system was simultaneously heavily loaded and, uniquely, in slight net balance. Pressure in this
system is a property of flow direction, not of caseload magnitude, and it has been absent since 2024.
The predictability question produced the study's most instructive negative result. Under a leakage-free
chronological evaluation, a persistence forecast that uses no model at all achieved MAE 12.0 and R² 0.993,
while the best fitted model achieved MAE 223.3 and a negative R². The result is fully explained by a
68.4% level shift between the training and test regimes, and it is reported rather than suppressed because
the same structural instability that defeats the models is the most important operational fact about the
system. The practical conclusion is not that forecasting is impossible here but that it must begin with
better data and regime-aware evaluation, and that until then, the honest deliverable is a range rather than
a point estimate.
9/27/26, 12:19 PM Capacity, Flow and Pressure Dynamics in the U.S. Unaccompanied Alien Children (UAC) Care System
file:///C:/Users/LOQ/OneDrive/Desktop/UAC Data Care Analysis/reports/research_paper.html 35/38
The three findings reinforce one another. A system whose caseload is discharge-governed, whose recent
trajectory is a 70% contraction, and whose flow direction flipped sign within the observation window is a
system in transition — and for a system in transition, the useful analytical output is not a projection but
a set of state indicators that let a manager see which way the transition is running. That is what the KPI
framework of Section 11, the pressure indicator of Section 12 and the deployed dashboard of Section 15 are
designed to provide.
The single highest-leverage recommendation arising from this study is not methodological. It is that a
federal care system which published its capacity denominator — bed counts, staffing ratios, caseworker
standards — alongside the caseload it already publishes would convert every relative measure in this paper
into an absolute one, would make the pressure construct answerable, and would materially improve the
forecastability of its own operations. Better data beats better models, and in this case the distance between
the two is measured in percentage points of a single published column.
